question:
A biased coin has the probability for a head $\frac{2}{3}$. The coin is tossed six times. Calculate the probability that:
(i) all six trials result in a tail;
(ii) none of the six results in a tail;
(iii) at least three heads appear in the six trials;
(iv) not more than three tails appear;
(v) the first two results in a head, and the remaining four in a tail;
(vi) The heads and tails appear alternatively. (you may not simplify the answer)

Two dice are tossed once, what is the probability that the:
(i) sum is less than $7$;
(ii) same number appeared;
(iii) difference is $2$;
(iv) product is greater than $8$;
(v) first number is even.

If $f(x) = mx^2$ for $0 \le x \le 2$ is a probability density function for a continuous random variable $x$, calculate:
(i) the value of $m$;
(ii) $P(0 \le x \le 1)$;
(iii) the mean;
(iv) the variance of the distribution.

On an average, two accidents occur every day on a busy highway and it is known that the number of accidents per day follows Poisson law. Find the probability that on a certain day:
(i) there are 2 accidents;
(ii) there is no accident;
(iii) Find the least value, $n$ such that the probability for $n$ accidents on a particular day is $< 0.1$. [Approximately take $e^{-2} = 1/7$]
